ium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/sauyon/devel/dotfiles/.drovr/wt/orca-cli-skills","sessionId":"d19ff9ff-38c8-4378-ac54-4f4061f74300","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"master"}
{"parentUuid":"ebbe1ee3-116a-472c-b22f-1e8c1ebdef9e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"b115785b-3194-4151-8381-11be3a344840","agentId":"a61e72dcd80d51e44","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MZyeNxLNWtPjr4xBaNYD9t","type":"tool_result","content":"acab1d5f orca: forward INT off a terminal, survive Ctrl-C on one; GUI dies with its launcher\nReview round 5. `trap : INT` swallowed every SIGINT, but the serve\nsupervisor forwards INT to its child before SIGKILLing it; now INT is\nforwarded unless the launcher's process group is the terminal's\nforeground group, which Ctrl-C already reaches.\n\nOn a terminal, Ctrl-C also reached bwrap, which died of it and\nSIGKILLed the payload (--die-with-parent) before its handler ran --\ntrue of the plain exec before this branch too. bwrap now starts with\nINT ignored (env --ignore-signal=INT); Node resets inherited\ndispositions and Chromium installs its own handler, so the payload\nstill gets it.\n\ndieWithParent = false is gone: the GUI's bwrap is the forwarding\nshell's child now, and that shell outlives `orca-ide open`; dying with\nit keeps a SIGKILLed launcher from orphaning Electron.\n\nA signal death is re-raised, so callers see the same status as a plain\nexec, and a trap racing the child's exit no longer reports the trap's\nstatus.\n\nTests: bwrap's own SigIgn (the old SIGINT case could not fail, Node\nresets its dispositions), INT forwarded off a terminal, Ctrl-C on a real\npty delivered once and handled (skipped without python3, as in CI), the\nGUI's --die-with-parent present and the payload outliving a parent that\nspawns it detached.\n\nComments: macKeybindings uses appimageContents; master's keybindings.json\ncomment moves onto the activation it describes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEntire-Checkpoint: f2105f7d01b2\n\n7db85d00 Merge origin/master into orca-cli-skills\norca.nix conflict: master's Mac keybinding table and activation, this\nbranch's CLI, skills and orcaGui binding -- unioned, one let, one attrset.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEntire-Checkpoint: 5b9b4283d7a3\n\n104b3c4f orca: forward TERM/HUP through bwrap, so `orca-ide serve` stops cleanly\nReview round 4. bwrap forwards no signals. Under the CLI's\n--die-with-parent, a TERM to `orca-ide serve` (systemd's\nKillMode=process unit, a plain kill) SIGKILLed the node supervisor\nbefore it could stop its server; with the GUI's dieWithParent = false,\nthe supervisor's own signals hit the GUI's bwrap and orphaned Electron\nserve, which kept the profile lock.\n\nBoth launchers -- the CLI's orca-ide and the GUI's inner orca-ide-app --\nnow run their bwrap in the background and forward TERM and HUP to\nbwrap's one child, the sandboxed process. `<&0` and env\n--default-signal=INT,QUIT undo what bash does to a background job, so\nstdin and Ctrl-C behave as before. Tests: TERM reaches a node-mode\nhandler inside the sandbox, stdin passes through, SIGINT is not\nignored, and the CLI uses the same launcher.\n\nAlso from round 4: the die-with-parent case requires a bwrap script,\nand the node-mode case compares stdout only.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEntire-Checkpoint: de6b0de03076\n\n37db3ccb orca: the GUI outlives the `orca-ide open` that starts it\nReview round 3 (Critical). wrapType2 defaults to --die-with-parent, and\nevery wrapper layer execs the next, so a GUI that `orca-ide open` spawns\ndetached is a bwrap whose parent is the CLI -- SIGKILLed the moment\n`open` returns. dieWithParent = false; a test case holds the launcher\nto it.\n\nThe node-mode case now checks printed output under a timeout: with the\nenv var lost on the way it would be a GUI launch, which a running\nOrca's single-instance lock answers with exit 0. Comment fixes: AppRun's\n--no-sandbox in node mode, appimageContents' users, the header's\ndescription of Orca's own ~/.local/bin symlink. --version matches whole\nwords.\n\nNot changed (nits): the test's skill list duplicates orcaSkills, so an\naddition is not checked; files a skill dir has beyond its manifest are\nnot flagged; a failed fetch realise shows up as \"missing\"; README's\ntest list; an empty /etc/hostname skips the uname -n fallback.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEntire-Checkpoint: 61a8d5ea6654\n\n4e0f5355 orca: keep --ozone-platform out of node mode; tests run under CI\nReview round 2. `orca-ide serve` probes ORCA_APP_EXECUTABLE with\nELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1, and Node rejected the GUI wrapper's always-on\n--ozone-platform=wayland (exit 9), silently dropping the orcad runtime.\nThe wrapper is hand-written now and adds the flag only outside node mode.\n\nTests: read /etc/hostname (what CI writes) with uname -n as the fallback\ninside Orca's sandbox, in both orca tests; jq instead of python3, which\nCI's tool shell lacks, hashing every file the manifest lists; hold\nORCA_APP_EXECUTABLE to exactly the profile's nixGL-wrapped GUI; a node-\nmode case for the probe above; tighter bwrap and guide-title matches.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEntire-Checkpoint: af092d532a0c\n\n777a68b5 orca: `orca-ide` is the CLI, the GUI is `orca-ide-app`\nReview round 1: Orca's skills tell agents outside an Orca terminal on\nLinux to run `orca-ide`, never bare `orca` -- here that started the GUI.\nMatch upstream's Linux install instead: orca-ide and orca are the CLI,\nthe GUI wrapper and desktop Exec move to orca-ide-app.\n\nORCA_APP_EXECUTABLE points `orca-ide open` at the nixGL-wrapped GUI;\nwithout it the CLI spawns the raw Electron in its own env, which has no\nwayland flag and exits on Hyprland.\n\nThe test now also holds the CLI launcher to being bwrap (run from an\nOrca terminal it is already inside an FHS env, so a bare wrapper would\npass), runs `skills get` under both names, and checks the skills rev is\nthe app's own sourceCommit. The rev is in the fetch's store name, so a\nrev bump without a hash bump fails instead of reusing the old tree.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEntire-Checkpoint: 253006912444\n\n9e53e6cc orca: install the CLI as `orca` and six of its agent skills\nThe app's own installer only reaches an Orca terminal (a PATH shim there,\nand a ~/.local/bin symlink to a raw store path). Run its CLI script in the\nGUI's FHS env as `orca`, so it works from any shell; the skills are stubs\nthat call `orca skills get`, so they need it.\n\nSkills come from stablyai/orca at e6f7fba5, the commit this AppImage was\nbuilt from; tests/orca-cli-skills.sh holds them to the sha256s in the\nbuild's own current-manifest.json.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEntire-Checkpoint: d04b04f0be26\n\ncommit acab1d5fd4c644c09c28c5d7766f5ad3c8af3147\nAuthor: Sauyon Lee <git@sjle.co>\nDate:   Sat Oct 24 12:01:02 2026 +0000\n\n    orca: forward INT off a terminal, survive Ctrl-C on one; GUI dies with its launcher\n    \n    Review round 5. `trap : INT` swallowed every SIGINT, but the serve\n    supervisor forwards INT to its child before SIGKILLing it; now INT is\n    forwarded unless the launcher's process group is the terminal's\n    foreground group, which Ctrl-C already reaches.\n    \n    On a terminal, Ctrl-C also reached bwrap, which died of it and\n    SIGKILLed the payload (--die-with-parent) before its handler ran --\n    true of the plain exec before this branch too. bwrap now starts with\n    INT ignored (env --ignore-signal=INT); Node resets inherited\n    dispositions and Chromium installs its own handler, so the payload\n    still gets it.\n    \n    dieWithParent = false is gone: the GUI's bwrap is the forwarding\n    shell's child now, and that shell outlives `orca-ide open`; dying with\n    it keeps a SIGKILLed launcher from orphaning Electron.\n    \n    A signal death is re-raised, so callers see the same status as a plain\n    exec, and a trap racing the child's exit no longer reports the trap's\n    status.\n    \n    Tests: bwrap's own SigIgn (the old SIGINT case could not fail, Node\n    resets its dispositions), INT forwarded off a terminal, Ctrl-C on a real\n    pty delivered once and handled (skipped without python3, as in CI), the\n    GUI's --die-with-parent present and the payload outliving a parent that\n    spawns it detached.\n    \n    Comments: macKeybindings uses appimageContents; master's keybindings.json\n    comment moves onto the activation it describes.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n    Entire-Checkpoint: f2105f7d01b2\n\n orca.nix                 |  75 +++++++++++++++++++------------\n tests/orca-cli-skills.sh | 114 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------------\n 2 files changed, 131 insertions(+), 58 deletions(-)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e58dccb8-0d8d-4506-a9ce-86ace8005d3d","timestamp":"2026-10-24T12:01:29.621Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"acab1d5f orca: forward INT off a terminal, survive Ctrl-C on one; GUI dies with its launcher\nReview round 5. `trap : INT` swallowed every SIGINT, but the serve\nsupervisor forwards INT to its child before SIGKILLing it; now INT is\nforwarded unless the launcher's process group is the terminal's\nforeground group, which Ctrl-C already reaches.\n\nOn a terminal, Ctrl-C also reached bwrap, which died of it and\nSIGKILLed the payload (--die-with-parent) before its handler ran --\ntrue of the plain exec before this branch too. bwrap now starts with\nINT ignored (env --ignore-signal=INT); Node resets inherited\ndispositions and Chromium installs its own handler, so the payload\nstill gets it.\n\ndieWithParent = false is gone: the GUI's bwrap is the forwarding\nshell's child now, and that shell outlives `orca-ide open`; dying with\nit keeps a SIGKILLed launcher from orphaning Electron.\n\nA signal death is re-raised, so callers see the same status as a plain\nexec, and a trap racing the child's exit no longer reports the trap's\nstatus.\n\nTests: bwrap's o