{"parentUuid":"ca1b38f7-b36e-4cff-8c16-78096913ab2b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"b115785b-3194-4151-8381-11be3a344840","agentId":"a61e72dcd80d51e44","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EtKhvGyPAENvMHKhoVf4Ni","type":"tool_result","content":"commit acab1d5fd4c644c09c28c5d7766f5ad3c8af3147\nAuthor: Sauyon Lee <git@sjle.co>\nDate:   Sat Oct 24 12:01:02 2026 +0000\n\n    orca: forward INT off a terminal, survive Ctrl-C on one; GUI dies with its launcher\n    \n    Review round 5. `trap : INT` swallowed every SIGINT, but the serve\n    supervisor forwards INT to its child before SIGKILLing it; now INT is\n    forwarded unless the launcher's process group is the terminal's\n    foreground group, which Ctrl-C already reaches.\n    \n    On a terminal, Ctrl-C also reached bwrap, which died of it and\n    SIGKILLed the payload (--die-with-parent) before its handler ran --\n    true of the plain exec before this branch too. bwrap now starts with\n    INT ignored (env --ignore-signal=INT); Node resets inherited\n    dispositions and Chromium installs its own handler, so the payload\n    still gets it.\n    \n    dieWithParent = false is gone: the GUI's bwrap is the forwarding\n    shell's child now, and that shell outlives `orca-ide open`; dying with\n    it keeps a SIGKILLed launcher from orphaning Electron.\n    \n    A signal death is re-raised, so callers see the same status as a plain\n    exec, and a trap racing the child's exit no longer reports the trap's\n    status.\n    \n    Tests: bwrap's own SigIgn (the old SIGINT case could not fail, Node\n    resets its dispositions), INT forwarded off a terminal, Ctrl-C on a real\n    pty delivered once and handled (skipped without python3, as in CI), the\n    GUI's --die-with-parent present and the payload outliving a parent that\n    spawns it detached.\n    \n    Comments: macKeybindings uses appimageContents; master's keybindings.json\n    comment moves onto the activation it describes.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n    Entire-Checkpoint: f2105f7d01b2\n\ndiff --git a/tests/orca-cli-skills.sh b/tests/orca-cli-skills.sh\nindex f5e2a0df..afdf58a0 100755\n--- a/tests/orca-cli-skills.sh\n+++ b/tests/orca-cli-skills.sh\n@@ -80,32 +80,80 @@ if [ \"$out\" = node-ok ]; then\n else fail \"orca-ide-app node mode\" \"${out:-$(tail -1 \"$T/node-err\")}\"; fi\n \n # Stopping `orca-ide serve` (systemctl, its own supervisor, a plain kill)\n-# signals the outermost pid. bwrap neither forwards signals nor dies when\n-# --die-with-parent is off, so without a forwarding launcher the TERM kills\n-# bwrap and the process inside keeps the profile lock. Node mode stands in for\n-# the GUI here: same launcher, and it can report what it saw. The script exits\n-# on its own after 20s, so a regression cannot leak it.\n-ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 \"$GUI/bin/orca-ide-app\" -e '\n-  process.on(\"SIGTERM\", () => { console.log(\"got-term\"); process.exit(0); });\n-  console.log(\"ready\"); setTimeout(() => process.exit(3), 20000);' >\"$T/term\" 2>&1 &\n-w=$!\n-for _ in $(seq 150); do grep -q ready \"$T/term\" && break; sleep 0.2; done\n-kill -TERM \"$w\" 2>/dev/null; wait \"$w\"; rc=$?\n-if grep -q got-term \"$T/term\" && [ \"$rc\" -eq 0 ]; then\n-  pass \"TERM to the GUI launcher reaches the process in the sandbox\"\n-else fail \"signal forwarding\" \"rc=$rc, saw: $(tr '\\n' ' ' < \"$T/term\")\"; fi\n+# signals the outermost pid, and bwrap forwards nothing: without a forwarding\n+# launcher a TERM kills bwrap and the process inside keeps the profile lock.\n+# Node mode stands in for the GUI here: same launcher, and it can report what\n+# it saw. Each payload exits on its own after 20s, so a regression cannot leak\n+# it. Started via env --default-signal, as Node's spawn would: a background\n+# job of this non-interactive shell would otherwise begin with SIGINT ignored,\n+# which bash then cannot trap.\n+payload() { # payload <out-file>: node-mode GUI launch reporting TERM and INT\n+  ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 env --default-signal=INT \"$GUI/bin/orca-ide-app\" -e '\n+    for (const s of [\"SIGTERM\", \"SIGINT\"])\n+      process.on(s, () => { console.log(\"got-\" + s); process.exit(0); });\n+    console.log(\"ready\"); setTimeout(() => process.exit(3), 20000);' >\"$1\" 2>&1 &\n+  w=$!\n+  for _ in $(seq 150); do grep -q ready \"$1\" && break; sleep 0.2; done\n+}\n \n-# The forwarding launcher backgrounds bwrap, which in a non-interactive shell\n-# means stdin from /dev/null and SIGINT ignored -- the CLI reads stdin, and\n-# Ctrl-C must still kill it. Run in the foreground here, so neither is the\n-# test's own doing.\n-got=$(echo stdin-ok | ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 \"$GUI/bin/orca-ide-app\" -e 'process.stdin.pipe(process.stdout)' 2>&1)\n+payload \"$T/term\"\n+# While it runs: bwrap ignores SIGINT. A terminal's Ctrl-C goes to the whole\n+# foreground group, and a bwrap that died of it would SIGKILL the payload\n+# (--die-with-parent) before its own SIGINT handler finished.\n+bw=$(cat /proc/\"$w\"/task/*/children 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $1}')\n+ign=$(awk '/^SigIgn:/ {print $2}' /proc/\"$bw\"/status 2>/dev/null)\n+if [ -n \"$ign\" ] && [ $(( 0x$ign & 2 )) -ne 0 ]; then pass \"the launcher's bwrap ignores SIGINT\"\n+else fail \"SIGINT\" \"bwrap ($bw) SigIgn=${ign:-unreadable}\"; fi\n+kill -TERM \"$w\" 2>/dev/null; wait \"$w\"; rc=$?\n+if grep -q got-SIGTERM \"$T/term\" && [ \"$rc\" -eq 0 ]; then\n+  pass \"TERM to the GUI launcher reaches the process in the sandbox\"\n+else fail \"TERM forwarding\" \"rc=$rc, saw: $(tr '\\n' ' ' < \"$T/term\")\"; fi\n+\n+# The serve supervisor forwards SIGINT to its child too; off a terminal the\n+# launcher must pass it on, not swallow it.\n+payload \"$T/int\"\n+kill -INT \"$w\" 2>/dev/null; wait \"$w\"; rc=$?\n+if grep -q got-SIGINT \"$T/int\" && [ \"$rc\" -eq 0 ]; then\n+  pass \"INT to the GUI launcher (no terminal) reaches the process in the sandbox\"\n+else fail \"INT forwarding\" \"rc=$rc, saw: $(tr '\\n' ' ' < \"$T/int\")\"; fi\n+\n+# Ctrl-C itself, on a real pty: the payload sees exactly one SIGINT and gets\n+# to handle it. Needs python3 for the pty, which CI's tool shell lacks.\n+if command -v python3 >/dev/null; then\n+  got=$(timeout 60 python3 -I - \"$GUI/bin/orca-ide-app\" <<'EOF'\n+import os, pty, select, sys, time\n+pid, fd = pty.fork()\n+if pid == 0:\n+    os.environ[\"ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE\"] = \"1\"\n+    os.execv(sys.argv[1], [sys.argv[1], \"-e\",\n+        'let n = 0; process.on(\"SIGINT\", () => { n++; setTimeout(() => {'\n+        ' console.log(\"ints=\" + n); process.exit(0); }, 1500); });'\n+        ' console.log(\"ready\"); setTimeout(() => process.exit(3), 20000);'])\n+buf, t = b\"\", time.time()\n+def read_until(s, secs):\n+    global buf\n+    t = time.time()\n+    while s not in buf and time.time() - t < secs:\n+        try:\n+            if select.select([fd], [], [], 0.5)[0]: buf += os.read(fd, 1024)\n+        except OSError: break\n+read_until(b\"ready\", 30); os.write(fd, b\"\\x03\"); read_until(b\"ints=\", 10)\n+import re\n+m = re.findall(r\"ints=\\d+\", buf.decode(errors=\"replace\"))  # the echoed ^C precedes it\n+print(m[-1] if m else \"none\", os.waitpid(pid, 0)[1])\n+EOF\n+)\n+  if [ \"$got\" = \"ints=1 0\" ]; then pass \"Ctrl-C on a pty reaches the payload once, and it exits cleanly\"\n+  else fail \"Ctrl-C\" \"got $got (want ints=1, status 0)\"; fi\n+else\n+  echo \"skip Ctrl-C on a pty: no python3\"\n+fi\n+\n+# A background job's stdin is /dev/null unless the launcher says otherwise,\n+# and the CLI reads stdin.\n+got=$(echo stdin-ok | ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 timeout 60 \"$GUI/bin/orca-ide-app\" -e 'process.stdin.pipe(process.stdout)' 2>&1)\n if [ \"$got\" = stdin-ok ]; then pass \"the launcher passes stdin through\"\n else fail \"stdin\" \"got '$got'\"; fi\n-ign=$(ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 \"$GUI/bin/orca-ide-app\" -p \\\n-  'require(\"fs\").readFileSync(\"/proc/self/status\",\"utf8\").match(/SigIgn:\\s*(\\w+)/)[1]' 2>&1)\n-if [ -n \"$ign\" ] && [ $(( 0x$ign & 2 )) -eq 0 ]; then pass \"SIGINT is not ignored inside the launcher\"\n-else fail \"SIGINT\" \"SigIgn=$ign\"; fi\n \n # The CLI uses the same launcher (it is what systemctl stops for a service\n # `orca-ide serve`), wrapping its own bwrap.\n@@ -114,13 +162,21 @@ if grep -q 'forward-signals' \"$CLI/bin/orca-ide\" 2>/dev/null \\\n   pass \"the CLI forwards signals into its sandbox too\"\n else fail \"CLI launcher\" \"$CLI/bin/orca-ide is not the forwarding launcher\"; fi\n \n-# `orca-ide open` spawns the GUI detached, but each wrapper layer execs the\n-# next, so the GUI's bwrap is the CLI's direct child; with --die-with-parent\n-# it is SIGKILLed the moment `open` returns.\n+# `orca-ide open` spawns the GUI detached and returns. The launcher shell\n+# outlives that parent, and the GUI's bwrap is the launcher's child, so\n+# --die-with-parent stays on: it is what takes the GUI down if the launcher\n+# is SIGKILLed (the serve supervisor's last resort) instead of orphaning it.\n gui_launcher=$(readlink -f \"$GUI/bin/.orca-ide-unwrapped\")\n-if grep -q '/bin/bwrap' \"$gui_launcher\" 2>/dev/null && ! grep -q -- '--die-with-parent' \"$gui_launcher\"; then\n-  pass \"the GUI outlives the orca-ide open that started it\"\n-else fail \"GUI launcher\" \"$gui_launcher passes --die-with-parent (or is missing)\"; fi\n+if grep -q '/bin/bwrap' \"$gui_launcher\" 2>/dev/null && grep -q -- '--die-with-parent' \"$gui_launcher\"; then\n+  pass \"the GUI's bwrap dies with its launcher\"\n+else fail \"GUI launcher\" \"$gui_launcher lacks --die-with-parent (or is missing)\"; fi\n+# And the payload outlives a parent that spawns it detached and exits.\n+bash -c 'ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 setsid \"$1\" -e \"\n+  setTimeout(() => require(\\\"fs\\\").writeFileSync(process.argv[1], \\\"alive\\\"), 3000);\n+  setTimeout(() => process.exit(0), 4000);\" \"$2\" >/dev/null 2>&1 &' _ \"$GUI/bin/orca-ide-app\" \"$T/alive\"\n+for _ in $(seq 75); do [ -s \"$T/alive\" ] && break; sleep 0.2; done\n+if [ -s \"$T/alive\" ]; then pass \"the GUI outlives the parent that spawned it (orca-ide open)\"\n+else fail \"GUI lifetime\" \"payload died with its spawning parent\"; fi\n \n # The manifest travels with the extracted AppImage, which the CLI's runScript\n # references, so it is in the CLI's closure.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d5978825-bd10-485c-a895-987148188f4b","timestamp":"2026-10-24T12:02:31.653Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"commit acab1d5fd4c644c09c28c5d7766f5ad3c8af3147\nAuthor: Sauyon Lee <git@sjle.co>\nDate:   Sat Oct 24 12:01:02 2026 +0000\n\n    orca: forward INT off a terminal, survive Ctrl-C on one; GUI dies with its launcher\n    \n    Review round 5. `trap : INT` swallowed every SIGINT, but the serve\n    supervisor forwards INT to its child before SIGKILLing it; now INT is\n    forwarded unless the launcher's process group is the terminal's\n    foreground group, which Ctrl-C already reaches.\n    \n    On a terminal, Ctrl-C also reached bwrap, which died of it and\n    SIGKILLed the payload (--die-with-parent) before its handler ran --\n    true of the plain exec before this branch too. bwrap now starts with\n    INT ignored (env --ignore-signal=INT); Node resets inherited\n    dispositions and Chromium installs its own handler, so the payload\n    still gets it.\n    \n    dieWithParent = false is gone: the GUI's bwrap is the forwarding\n    shell's child now, and that shell outlives `orca-ide open`; dying with\n    it keeps a SIGKILLed launcher from orphaning Electron.\n    \n    A signal death is re-raised, so callers see the same status as a plain\n    exec, and a trap racing the child's exit no longer reports the trap's\n    status.\n    \n    Tests: bwrap's own SigIgn (the old SIGINT case could not fail, Node\n    resets its dispositions), INT forwarded off a terminal, Ctrl-C on a real\n    pty delivered once and handled (skipped without python3, as in CI), the\n    GUI's --die-with-parent